  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "How Bengali script works",
      "body": "<p>Like Devanagari, the Bengali script is an <i>abugida</i>: every consonant letter already carries a vowel. In Bangla that vowel is a rounded o, so <span lang=\"bn\">ক</span> is ko. Other vowels are written with signs attached to the consonant, and the hasanta (<span lang=\"bn\">ক্</span>) removes the vowel entirely.</p>\n<p>Most letters hang from a headline, the <i>matra</i>, which joins up across a word: <span lang=\"bn\">বাংলা</span>. Some letters, like <span lang=\"bn\">এ ঐ ও ঔ ঙ ঞ ৎ</span>, have no headline, and a few have only half of one.</p>\n<p>The consonants come in the same rows of five as Devanagari, from the throat to the lips. If you already read Hindi, many letters will look like cousins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "The disappearing o",
      "body": "<p>The built-in o is often not said, especially at the end of a word. The spelling does not tell you; you learn it word by word.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">কলম</td><td>ko-lo-mo on paper, said kolom (pen)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">বাংলা</td><td>said bangla, with no vowel after ং</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Sometimes the vowel is said o rather than ɔ, as in the first syllable of <span lang=\"bn\">কলম</span>. The recordings follow the usual pronunciation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Conjunct letters (juktakkhor)",
      "body": "<p>Two consonants with no vowel between them are fused into one shape, often stacked one on top of the other:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">ক্ + ষ = ক্ষ</td><td>said kh, as in <span lang=\"bn\">ক্ষমা</span> (forgiveness)</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">ন্ + ত = ন্ত</td><td>nt</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">জ্ + ঞ = জ্ঞ</td><td>said gg, as in <span lang=\"bn\">জ্ঞান</span> (knowledge)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>A য after a consonant becomes the small hook <span lang=\"bn\">্য</span> and usually doubles the consonant or changes the vowel: <span lang=\"bn\">ব্যাঙ</span> is byang. A র before a consonant becomes a small stroke on top (<span lang=\"bn\">র্ক</span>), and after one it becomes a small mark below (<span lang=\"bn\">প্র</span>).</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Bengali digits",
      "body": "<p>Bangla has its own digits, still used everywhere from prices to page numbers:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">০ ১ ২ ৩ ৪</td><td>0 1 2 3 4</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"bn\">৫ ৬ ৭ ৮ ৯</td><td>5 6 7 8 9</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Watch out for <span lang=\"bn\">৪</span>, which is 4 even though it looks like an 8.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
